{
  "requirement": "After `habeas refactor` succeeds, each record it changed MUST pass or fail\nvalidation exactly as it did before the move.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The obligation is that every record `habeas refactor` changed passes or fails validation after the move exactly as it did before. That gives two testable outcomes: pass before and pass after, and fail before and fail after with the same errors. The tests cover both across the record kinds the refactor rewrites.\n\n**Pass before, pass after.** Each test checks validation is clean before the move, runs `refactor`, then asserts validation is clean after (or that the only errors are on the glob record `evidence/rules.check.json`, whose match set changed by design):\n- Moved function: `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_function_keeps_its_evidence_current]` asserts `errors(root)` is empty after the move. It also asserts that unaffected files stay byte-identical and that a second run changes nothing.\n- Moved requirement: `fn[a_moved_requirement_renames_its_references_and_evidence]` checks that only the expected files differ and that the remaining errors are all on the glob record.\n- Moved file: `fn[a_moved_file_rewrites_anchors_and_paths]`.\n- Doc-comment hash: `fn[a_rename_rewrites_the_hash_of_a_node_whose_doc_comment_names_it]` checks that the hash of a node whose doc comment names the renamed requirement is recomputed, so the record stays current.\n- Moved term: `fn[a_moved_term_rewrites_definition_inputs_and_the_texts_using_it]`.\n- Moved chapter, with glob selections and their matches: `fn[a_moved_chapter_rewrites_glob_selections_and_their_matches]`.\n- Citation review: `fn[a_citation_review_follows_its_item_and_the_name_it_cites]` and `fn[a_citation_reviews_commentary_follows_a_renamed_requirement]`.\n- Site review: `src/refactor/tests.rs#fn[site_reviews_follow_their_site_and_renamed_requirement]` compares site errors before and after.\n- Versioned SDK-dependency records: `fn[versioned_records_preserve_sdk_dependencies_across_verified_moves]` runs the scenarios rename, move, selector, file and extra. Each asserts that the migrated record equals an independently built expected record and that validation is clean.\n\n**Fail before, fail after.**\n- `fn[a_record_failing_before_the_move_fails_the_same_way_after_it]` sets `determination` to false, records the errors, and moves the function. It asserts that the post-move errors equal the pre-move errors, with only the rewritten path substituted.\n- `fn[a_moved_example_rewrites_its_example_field]` does the same for an example record that fails because no test covers it. It compares the error lists before and after the rename.\n- In the `edit` and `bad-extra` scenarios of the versioned test, a record that is stale or has a bad dependency before the move is left unchanged. It still fails afterwards, and the test asserts both.\n\n**Records not changed.** Records that were not changed are outside this obligation. `fn[a_citation_review_whose_citations_changed_in_the_move_is_left_unchanged]` asserts that such a record stays byte-identical and is not moved.\n\nThe assertions compare concrete validation error lists or require empty ones, so an implementation that changed a record's pass or fail status would be caught. All the tests carry `{test}` references to `requirements.refactor.equivalence`. I found no unmarked relevant tests and no uncovered scenarios.",
  "references": "0e52393f5a979038f763afb093606bdb5f95b58e321dc41a8544c214c422188e",
